#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# assoc

#% text
# `assoc(k1 -> v1, k2 -> v2, ...)` gives an association: a map from the keys `k1`, `k2`, ... to the values `v1`, `v2`, ....

#% text
# `assoc([k1 -> v1, ...])` makes the association from a list of rules.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Keys and values may be any terms: strings, numbers, symbols, lists.

#% item
# Entries keep the order in which they were given. A repeated key keeps its first position and its last value.

#% item
# `a[k]` gives the value of key `k`; `lookup(a, k, default)` gives a default for a missing key.

#% item
# `keys`, `values`, `to_rules`, `insert`, `delete_key`, `key_exists`, `map_values` and `merge` work with associations; `length` counts the entries.

#% item
# Lookups are fast also for large associations: the keys are indexed.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
a = assoc("x" -> 1, "y" -> 2)

#% cell
a["y"]
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
assoc([1 -> "one", 2 -> "two"])
#% output
#= assoc(1 -> "one", 2 -> "two")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A repeated key keeps its first position and its last value:

#% cell
assoc("a" -> 1, "b" -> 2, "a" -> 3)
#% output
#= assoc("a" -> 3, "b" -> 2)

#% text
# Keys of any kind:

#% cell
assoc(x -> 1, [1, 2] -> "pair", 2.5 -> true)
#% output
#= assoc(x -> 1, [1, 2] -> "pair", 2.5 -> true)

#% text
# An empty association:

#% cell
assoc()
#% output
#= assoc()

#% text
# Patterns of associations match associations:

#% cell
rewrite(f(assoc(1 -> 2, 3 -> 4)), f(assoc(k_ -> v_, ___)) :> [k, v])
#% output
#= [1, 2]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Named parameters of a model, kept together and read by name:

#% cell
model = assoc("lam" -> 4.0e-21, "f" -> 1.46e11, "nc" -> 3)
model["lam"]*model["nc"]
#% output
#= 1.1999999999999998e-20

#% text
# Counting words:

#% cell
counts(string_split("the cat and the hat and the bat"))
#% output
#= assoc("the" -> 3, "cat" -> 1, "and" -> 2, "hat" -> 1, "bat" -> 1)

#% text
# A table of values that grows one entry at a time:

#% cell
{
  var squares = assoc()
  for k in range(4) { squares = insert(squares, k -> k^2) }
  squares
}
#% output
#= assoc(1 -> 1, 2 -> 4, 3 -> 9, 4 -> 16)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `keys` and `values` take an association apart, `to_rules` gives its rules:

#% cell
[keys(a), values(a), to_rules(a)]
#% output
#= [["x", "y"], [1, 2], ["x" -> 1, "y" -> 2]]

#% text
# The number of entries:

#% cell
length(a)
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# An association is a value; `insert` makes a new one:

#% cell
b = insert(a, "z" -> 3)
[a, b]
#% output
#= [assoc("x" -> 1, "y" -> 2), assoc("x" -> 1, "y" -> 2, "z" -> 3)]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A missing key gives a failure; give a default with `lookup`:

#% cell
a["w"]
#% output
#! failure(missing_key, "w")

#% cell
lookup(a, "w", 0)
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `keys`, `values`, `lookup`, `insert`, `merge`, `counts`, `group_by`
